.TH DANBY-SLITS 1
.SH NAME
danby-slits \- the double slit, actually solved
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/slits
.br
.B danby/slits -c
.RB [ -one ]
.RB [ -s
.IR separation ]
.RB [ -w
.IR width ]
.RB [ -v
.IR height ]
.SH MODEL
.IR Danby-twoslit (1)
draws the interference pattern from a formula: it assumes the answer's
shape and fills in the numbers.  This solves the time-dependent
Schrodinger equation on a grid and lets the pattern happen \(em one
packet goes in, meets a wall with two gaps in it, and what comes out the
far side is worked out rather than assumed.
.PP
Both are worth having.  The formula is exact and instant and says
nothing about how the pattern arrives; this is approximate and slow and
shows the wave going through BOTH slits at once, which is the part with
no classical account and the part a formula cannot draw.
.SH DESCRIPTION
The equation is
.BR /lib/danby/slits.pde ,
read at startup, so it can be changed without a compiler.  See
.IR pde (2)
for the language.
.SS The screen accumulates
The strip on the right is the screen, and it adds up.  Each step
contributes however much probability is passing that column at that
moment, so the fringes build over the run the way a real pattern builds
from arrivals \(em rather than appearing complete.
.SS What to look for
Press
.B o
and the second slit is blocked.  Everything else stays the same, and the
pattern collapses to a single broad lobe sitting on the slit that is
still open.  Press it again and the fringes come back.
.PP
That is the whole thing.  With both slits open the BRIGHTEST point on
the screen is midway between them \(em directly behind the wall, where
nothing came through at all.  Measured: the maximum sits at y = 9.60,
which is the midpoint, and with one slit blocked it moves to y = 11.40,
which is the open slit and where a classical particle would have gone.
.SS Controls
.B o
blocks and unblocks a slit;
.B s
and
.B S
change their separation,
.B w
and
.B W
their width,
.B v
and
.B V
the height of the wall.  Any of them restarts, since all of them are in
the equation or the initial condition.  Space pauses, r restarts, q
quits.
.SS Checking it without a display
.B -c
runs the problem, prints the accumulated screen profile as a bar chart
and exits without opening anything.
.B -one
blocks a slit, and
.BR -s ,
.B -w
and
.B -v
set the three numbers, so the comparison the program is for can be made
where there is no display.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /lib/danby/slits.pde
The equation.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/slits.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby (1),
.IR danby-twoslit (1),
.IR danby-tdse (1),
.IR slitstest (1),
.IR pde (2)
.SH BUGS
A wall is only a wall if the packet cannot get through it.  The first
version used a height of 30 against a packet energy of 18, and about
three per cent tunnelled through EVERYWHERE along it; since the packet
is broad, that leak was comparable to what came through the slits, and
the pattern carried the packet's own shape rather than the slits'.  It
looked like interference and was not.  The wall is 90 now, and the
difference is visible in the one-slit case: two lobes at 30, one at 90.
.PP
The boundary is clamped, so anything reaching an edge comes back.  The
run is sized so the interesting part happens first.
.PP
Blocking a slit moves it out of the domain rather than closing it.  The
two slits are ADDED in the potential, so setting both to the same place
gives 2 where they overlap, and 1\-2 is a well rather than a hole.
.PP
This is a wave in two dimensions and not a stream of particles.  It
shows the pattern forming; it does not show anything arriving at a
point, which is the other half of what makes the double slit strange.
.PP
At 240 by 192 a step costs about 90ms, so it runs at ten frames a
second or so.  It is a simulation being watched, not an animation.
